4 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186447578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447624 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ea6eed03-9831-52dd-806d-2cce8dda7014" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447627 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447627 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105744" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447627 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447627 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨3,-1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,4⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=4$ and $f(b)=-2,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447627 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04ad605f-8ccb-55ea-8b18-2d7ae6e4a34a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447627 #edb/ref 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)·(b-a) \\\\ -2-4 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨1,4⟩-⟨3,-1⟩) \\\\ -6 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,5⟩ \\\\ -6 & =-2{f}_{x}(c)+5{f}_{y}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447639 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447639 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130836" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447639 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447639 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨-1,-1,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,0,-2⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=5$ and $f(b)=-1,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447639 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c15a8036-6858-5a29-a56c-8e96a5ccf287" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447639 #edb/ref 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ -1-5 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨3,0,-2⟩-⟨-1,-1,3⟩) \\\\ -6 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨4,1,-5⟩ \\\\ -6 & =4{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)-5{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $4{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)-5{f}_{z}(c)=-6.$ *" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447640 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447640 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36826" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447640 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447640 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}+xy+2{x}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,4⟩,$ respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447640 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cde771f2-0ae1-5186-919d-63d0f22b095d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447640 #edb/ref 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}+xy+2{x}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,4)-f(0,3) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨3,4⟩-⟨0,3⟩) \\\\ 3(4)^{2}+3(4)+2(3)^{2}-(3(3)^{2}+0(3)+2(0)^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4x+y,x+6y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}51 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =⟨4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =12{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}+{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2} \\\\ 51 & =13{c}_{1}+9{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-3}{4-3} & =\\frac{x-0}{3-0} \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $13{c}_{1}+9{c}_{2}=51,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13{c}_{1}+9(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3) & =51 \\\\ 13{c}_{1}+3{c}_{1}+27 & =51 \\\\ 16{c}_{1} & =24 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{2}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3=\\frac{7}{2}.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484807 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186485598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484807 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186485606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484807 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186485621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484807 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186485627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484807 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186485643 #edb/ref 1759218604541