 17592186045417] [17592186485463 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485463 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485463 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485463 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485463 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485463 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186484865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485463 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dcf7b736-691e-5d1f-a95e-1aa8428199b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485572 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485572 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36836" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485572 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485572 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)=5{y}^{2}-2x$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3⟩,$ respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485572 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b6c8dce9-3855-5452-ba32-6512e799f65b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485572 #edb/ref 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=5{y}^{2}-2x$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3)-f(0,2) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,3⟩-⟨0,2⟩) \\\\ (5(3)^{2}-2(2))-(5(2)^{2}-2(0)) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-2,10y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-2,10{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =⟨-2,10{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =-4+10{c}_{2} \\\\ 10{c}_{2} & =25 \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{3-2} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ x & =2y-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2{c}_{2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2},$ we obtain that ${c}_{1}=2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}-4=1.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(1,\\frac{5}{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485598 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186485621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485598 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "090604db-a13f-5a31-aa02-93d119fcd0b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485598 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186362602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485606 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "26ecae40-501e-554b-8721-b010b2c744c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485606 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186485525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485621 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186485606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485621 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "69751aa3-ae26-5bd3-a4e3-94ae9355433d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485621 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186399517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485627 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186485643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485627 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "94e32dde-ff91-50a9-ab93-70a32c94ccab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485627 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186485572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485643 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186485598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485643 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fafd9199-788c-513d-ba61-5620b21205a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485643 #edb/r